# Horse Rasterizer Renderer Plan

> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.

**Goal:** Make Horse a single-backend, high-performance rasterized renderer with strong lighting, visibility, shadows, ambient lighting, reflections, GI and volumetrics while keeping game-specific rendering systems outside this roadmap.

**Architecture:** `Renderer::Rasterizer` is the only scene renderer registered by GAME. Rendering features are shared systems around it rather than alternate renderers. Every current renderer system gets a non-destructive global master override, and GAME's Debug UI exposes meaningful visual/performance controls instead of internal algorithm knobs. Internal tuning remains owned by Horse and is selected through quality presets.

**Tech Stack:** C++20, Horse, SDL3 GPU, SDL_shadercross, custom `c` build system.

**Spec:** User-approved renderer direction from 2026-09-18.

## Global Constraints

- Rasterizer remains the only scene renderer backend.
- Do not reintroduce selectable Ray Tracer or Path Tracer backends.
- No post-processing work is part of this roadmap unless separately requested.
- Keep Water, Decals, Particles, Terrain, Vegetation and other game-specific rendering systems out for now.
- Rendering systems must stay modular and backend-neutral where possible.
- Global system switches are non-destructive overrides. Turning a system off must not delete or rewrite ECS components; turning it back on restores the scene exactly as it was.
- Every implemented renderer system must have an `Enabled` master control where disabling it is meaningful.
- Debug controls must represent changes a player/developer can actually see or feel. Do not expose implementation trivia such as photon counts, probe dimensions, ray epsilon, internal sample budgets or BVH leaf sizes in the normal Renderer menu.
- Internal tuning stays in Horse and is selected through simple quality levels where useful: `Low`, `Medium`, `High`, `Ultra`.
- Do not add Debug UI for systems that do not exist yet.
- Camera controls follow the same philosophy: Active, Projection, FOV, Exposure, Tone Mapping, Render Distance, movement speed, sprint speed and mouse sensitivity. Hide low-value tuning unless needed for a concrete visual/debugging reason.
- Preserve the existing experimental BVH/viewport visualizer under Debug; it is not a player-facing renderer setting.
- Prefer quality loss only when the user explicitly selects a lower quality level. Do not silently degrade image quality to chase a target frame rate.

