## Phase 3 — Depth Prepass + Hi-Z

**Goal:** Build the visibility foundation without changing image quality.

```text
Depth Prepass
    ↓
full-resolution depth
    ↓
Hi-Z hierarchy
    ↓
conservative object-bound visibility tests
```

- Reuse Rasterizer depth.
- Only opaque geometry writes the occluder depth prepass; masked/blended geometry cannot accidentally become a solid occluder.
- Hi-Z stores conservative maximum depth for normal 0-near/1-far depth.
- Conservative occlusion only; false visible is acceptable, false hidden is not.
- Keep Frustum Culling independently switchable.

- [x] Add Depth Prepass ownership.
- [x] Build Hi-Z hierarchy on SDL_GPU compute.
- [ ] Add Hi-Z debug visualization/status under Debug/Experimental if it proves useful.

## Phase 4 — GPU Visibility + GPU-Driven Submission

**Goal:** Stop spending CPU/draw work on objects that cannot contribute to the frame.

Current first stage:

```text
CPU frustum entity mask
    ↓
opaque depth prepass
    ↓
Hi-Z
    ↓
GPU static-object AABB test
    ↓
refined entity visibility mask
    ↓
PBR vertex/fragment path
```

- Uses object/entity bounds, not triangle BVH nodes.
- Dynamic/skinned/deformed/parented/overlay entities stay conservatively visible in the first implementation.
- Current GPU visibility removes expensive shaded contribution but does not yet compact CPU draw calls.
- Preserve CPU fallback where backend capability requires it.

GPU-driven submission must not be implemented as a cosmetic swap from `SDL_DrawGPUPrimitives` to an indirect call. Horse's current raster draw representation still pushes a per-draw vertex offset through `VDraw.x`, indexes storage-backed vertices from `SV_VertexID`, and rebinds material textures per draw range. A useful GPU-driven path therefore needs a compact GPU draw list plus a material/draw batching representation that removes those CPU-side per-draw dependencies. Until that exists, keep the current explicit submission path rather than adding a misleading `GPU Driven` switch.

- [x] Add object bounds buffer.
- [x] Add GPU Hi-Z visibility compute pass.
- [ ] Add compact visible instance/draw list.
- [x] Connect GPU visibility results to Rasterizer shading through the existing entity visibility buffer.
- [ ] Redesign eligible raster batches so draw metadata and material selection can be consumed without per-draw CPU uniform/texture rebinding.
- [ ] Move eligible batches to indirect/GPU-driven submission so hidden objects also stop consuming draw/vertex work.

## Phase 5 — Forward+ / Clustered Lighting

**Goal:** Keep PBR cost scalable as scenes gain many lights.

The first implementation is tiled Forward+: 32x32 screen tiles with GPU-generated per-tile light lists. This deliberately establishes the scalable light-list path before adding depth slices. Depth-clustered refinement can be added later if measurements show the extra assignment complexity is useful.

```text
camera frustum
    ↓
32x32 screen tiles
    ↓
GPU light assignment
    ↓
per-tile light lists
    ↓
PBR shades only relevant lights
```

- Keep `LightComponent` API unchanged.
- Do not make game code understand tiles/clusters.
- Reuse Horse's existing packed shading/light buffer rather than introduce another light representation.
- Directional lights remain globally available.
- Point/spot lights are conservatively projected from their range and assigned only to overlapping screen tiles.
- Each tile currently stores up to 64 light indices. If a tile overflows, it is marked as overflow and PBR falls back to Horse's original full-light loop for that tile rather than dropping lighting.
- If Forward+ initialization or assignment fails, Rasterizer disables it and uses the original full-light loop.
- Forward+ is an internal rasterizer optimization, not another game-facing rendering system or normal Debug quality control.

**Status:** Forward+ HLSL/resource binding has been compiled and run successfully through the real GAME Metal backend. Stress testing with many point/spot lights is still outstanding.

